---
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
import { buildDocsJsonLd } from "../../../lib/docs-metadata"

const origin = Astro.site?.origin || "http://localhost:4321"
const title = "LyraShield Agent Plugin — Coding Agent Setup"
const description =
  "Install LyraShield in supported coding clients with the published CLI, or follow the client-specific setup guide."
const updatedDate = "2026-10-01"

// Kept in the same order as the launch-client table below.
const launchClients = [
  {
    name: "Claude Code",
    slug: "claude-code",
    path: "Claude marketplace",
    cliDefault: "Guided marketplace",
  },
  {
    name: "Cursor",
    slug: "cursor",
    path: "~/.cursor/plugins/local/lyrashield",
    cliDefault: "Direct local copy",
  },
  {
    name: "OpenAI Codex",
    slug: "openai-codex",
    path: "Codex marketplace",
    cliDefault: "Marketplace registration",
  },
  {
    name: "GitHub Copilot",
    slug: "github-copilot",
    path: "Copilot marketplace",
    cliDefault: "Guided marketplace",
  },
  {
    name: "Kiro",
    slug: "kiro",
    path: "Kiro Power in preparation; local MCP fallback available now",
    cliDefault: "MCP configuration fallback",
  },
]

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: "What is the LyraShield Agent Plugin?",
    a: "It is a portable package that carries the LyraShield MCP descriptor and security guidance. Each client still controls activation through its own documented local, marketplace or MCP settings path.",
  },
  {
    q: "Which clients have a native package path?",
    a: "The portable package has descriptors for Claude Code, Cursor, OpenAI Codex and GitHub Copilot. Kiro's native Power is the intended path on supported surfaces, but LyraShield's Power distribution is still in preparation and is not publicly installable; use the MCP settings fallback meanwhile. Other clients follow the client-specific setup in their guide.",
  },
  {
    q: "Do I need to log in before installing the plugin?",
    a: "Kiro's local stdio MCP fallback needs npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 login --oauth first. The Power package is not yet publicly available. Remote plugin paths complete hosted OAuth inside the client when the connection starts.",
  },
  {
    q: "Does the plugin let the agent change my code on its own?",
    a: "No. LyraShield findings and fixes are proposals requiring review before they land. A browser-confirmed OAuth delegation can run its recorded LyraShield workflows without repeated approval, but it remains limited by the user's current role, workspace, targets, profiles, budget, scope and connection expiry. LyraShield never auto-merges a pull request.",
  },
  {
    q: "Why does VS Code use an MCP config file instead of the plugin?",
    a: "No generated VS Code Agent Plugin shim exists. Its documented config path is .vscode/mcp.json. The published CLI can preview that installer with npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install vscode --dry-run, but writes are on hold pending the safe-writer fix. The VS Code guide documents the servers root key and per-entry stdio type. Runtime verification remains client-specific.",
  },
]

const { canonical, techArticle, breadcrumbList } = buildDocsJsonLd({
  origin,
  path: "/docs/integrations/agent-plugins",
  title,
  description,
  updatedDate,
  breadcrumbLabel: "Agent Plugins",
})
const jsonLd: unknown[] = [
  techArticle,
  breadcrumbList,
  {
    "@context": "https://schema.org",
    "@type": "FAQPage",
    mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
      "@type": "Question",
      name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { "@type": "Answer", text: item.a },
    })),
  },
]
---

<DocsLayout
  title={`${title} | LyraShield AI`}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  updatedDate={updatedDate}
  currentPath="/docs/integrations/agent-plugins"
>
  <h1>Agent Plugins</h1>
  <p>
    LyraShield ships as a portable plugin following the open{" "}
    <a href="https://agent-plugins.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
      >Agent Plugins v1.0.0</a
    >{" "}
    format. One package bundles two things: the LyraShield MCP server, which gives your agent the
    scan, finding, fix-plan and verify tools and a security skill that tells the agent when to
    reach for them. Each client still activates the package through its own supported path.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with the LyraShield CLI</h2>
  <p>
    The currently published npm CLI is{" "}
    <a href="https://www.npmjs.com/package/lyrashield/v/0.2.13" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
      version 0.2.13
    </a>
    . Its installer registry covers the clients listed below; newer integration guides can appear
    before their installers ship in the CLI. For other clients, use the linked guide's documented
    setup path and do not assume that a generic CLI command is available.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install claude-code --dry-run
npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install cursor --dry-run
npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install openai-codex --dry-run
npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install github-copilot --dry-run
npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install kiro --dry-run`}</code></pre>
  <p>
    These commands preview the published release's installer behavior without writing files. Keep
    <code>--dry-run</code>: config-writing installers in version <code>0.2.13</code> are on hold
    pending a safe-writer fix. Use each linked guide's manual steps or wait for a published CLI
    release that clears this gate. The published CLI's <code>init</code> command is likewise limited
    to the clients included in version <code>0.2.13</code>; preview it with{" "}
    <code>npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 init --dry-run</code>. A preview does not prove the client
    discovered or authenticated the integration.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>No credential in committed files:</strong> remote clients use hosted OAuth. Kiro's local
    stdio server reads <code>~/.lyrashield/credentials.json</code>. A workspace API key remains the
    explicit CI fallback; do not commit it.
  </div>

  <h2>Install paths in the published CLI</h2>
  <p>
    The portable source package contains client artifacts for the four plugin clients below. Kiro's
    native Power distribution is in preparation and is not publicly installable yet; its currently
    published CLI installer prints the MCP settings fallback. Other clients use the configuration,
    vendor CLI or guided-manual path documented in their own guide until an installer is included in
    a published CLI release.
  </p>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Client</th>
        <th>Activation</th>
        <th>Published CLI 0.2.13</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      {launchClients.map((client) => (
        <tr>
          <td><a href={`/docs/integrations/${client.slug}`}>{client.name}</a></td>
          <td><code>{client.path}</code></td>
          <td>{client.cliDefault}</td>
        </tr>
      ))}
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>VS Code uses MCP configuration:</strong> no generated Agent Plugin shim exists for VS
    Code. The published CLI can preview its config-file path, but do not apply config writes from
    version <code>0.2.13</code> while its safe-writer fix is pending. Preview the installer with{" "}
    <code>npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 install vscode --dry-run</code>; see the{" "}
    <a href="/docs/integrations/vscode">VS Code guide</a> for the current settings and manual path.
  </div>

  <h2>What the skill adds</h2>
  <p>
    The MCP server alone gives an agent tools; the bundled skill gives it judgement about depth. It
    maps a request like a pre-commit diff check to a quick bounded review and a deep security
    review to the intrusive agentic mode that only runs inside the authorised isolated sandbox for a
    repository target. That keeps a routine check from silently becoming a long expensive scan.
  </p>

  <h2>Findings are proposals, not automatic changes</h2>
  <p>
    Installing the plugin does not let an agent rewrite your code unattended. During remote OAuth,
    the user chooses one workspace and the requested workflows. Matching calls can then run without
    repeated LyraShield approval, while current role, target access, profile, budget, connection,
    scope, expiry and idempotency checks still apply. Mutating calls authenticated with an API key
    receive a connect_required response pointing at OAuth connect and out-of-grant delegated calls
    are denied. Historical <a href="/docs/approvals">exact-input approval records</a> are separate
    from active hosted delegation. Fixes and pull requests
    remain proposals requiring review before they land; LyraShield never merges them automatically.
  </p>

  <h2>Uninstall</h2>
  <p>
    Marketplace and UI installations must be removed through that client's own plugin manager.
    Preview CLI-managed removal only until the safe-writer fix is published.
  </p>
  <pre><code>npx --yes lyrashield@0.2.13 uninstall cursor --dry-run</code></pre>

  <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
  {faqItems.map((item) => (
    <>
      <h3>{item.q}</h3>
      <p>{item.a}</p>
    </>
  ))}

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Requirements:</strong> Node.js 24+ and a LyraShield account. LyraShield is live with
    open registration in open beta and some platform features remain on the near-term roadmap. Get
    started at{" "}
    <a href="https://lyrashieldai.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">lyrashieldai.com</a
    >.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
